.TH HSH 1 "April 2026" "Version 1.0" "Holberton School"
.SH NAME
hsh \- a simple UNIX command language interpreter (Simple Shell)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a basic command line interpreter that reads and executes commands from standard input or a file. It was developed as part of the Simple Shell project to replicate the core behavior of the standard sh (Bourne Shell).
.PP
.B Execution Modes:
.PP
\fBInteractive mode:\fR
If standard input is connected to a terminal, the shell displays the prompt \fB($) \fR and waits for user input. The shell reads the line (using \fBgetline\fR), tokenizes it into arguments, and executes the requested command.
.PP
\fBNon-interactive mode:\fR
If the shell is invoked via a pipe (e.g., \fBecho "ls" | ./hsh\fR) or reads from a file, it does not display a prompt. It executes commands silently and exits upon reaching the end of file (EOF).
.SH ERROR FORMAT
If a command is not found (neither as a built-in nor within the PATH), \fBhsh\fR prints an error message to the standard error stream (stderr) using the following format:
.PP
.I ./hsh: 1: command_name: not found
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
The following commands are built natively into the shell and do not require the creation of a new process (fork):
.TP
.B cd [directory]
Changes the current working directory of the process. If no argument is provided, the command defaults to the directory defined by the \fBHOME\fR environment variable. Upon failure (e.g., non-existent directory or insufficient permissions), an error message is printed to stderr.
.TP
.B env
Prints all current environment variables to the standard output.
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell gracefully with a status code of 0, ensuring all dynamically allocated memory is freed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.B hsh
relies on the following environment variables during execution:
.TP
.B PATH
Used to locate executable files when the entered command does not contain an absolute or relative path (a slash). The shell parses the colon-separated (:) directories in the PATH and dynamically builds the executable path.
.TP
.B HOME
Used by the \fBcd\fR built-in command as the default destination directory when no argument is provided.
